## 📘 PART 7 — Use Case Catalog

> One UC card per FC entry. If a single FC handles multiple distinct behavioral
> variants (different input discriminators → different code paths), create
> UC-[N]a, UC-[N]b, etc.

### 7.1 — UC Card Construction Rules

```
FOR EACH FC ROW, derive the following UC attributes:

  Actor:
    DERIVE FROM (priority order):
      1. OpenAPI @Operation(summary) or Swagger @Api(tags) on controller
      2. @CrossOrigin allowed origins
      3. Kafka consumer group ID → name of consuming service
      4. Integration test setup: who/what is simulated as caller
      5. README "Used By" or "Consumers" section
      6. If none: "Undetermined — see [controller file]"

  Precondition:
    DERIVE FROM:
      - @Valid / @Validated on request body → structural validity required
      - Guard clauses at method start: if (x == null) throw / return error
      - @PreAuthorize / security filter chain → auth requirement
      - @RequestParam(required=true) → required query params
      - Kafka: what state must exist before consumer processes message

  Flow (trace execution chain):
    STEP 1: Entry point (controller/handler receives input)
    STEP 2: Input parsing (what DTO is bound / deserialized)
    STEP 3: Validation layer (which validators are called, in what order)
    STEP 4: Business logic (which service methods are called)
    STEP 5: Data access (repository calls / cache reads / external HTTP)
    STEP 6: Response construction (what is built and returned)

    FORMAT: "[EntryClass:method] → [ValidatorClass:method] → [ServiceClass:method]
             → [RepositoryClass:method] → [ResponseDTO class]"
    Use + for parallel, → for sequential, branch with IF labels

  Postcondition:
    DERIVE FROM:
      - Return type of handler method
      - ResponseEntity<T> type argument
      - @ResponseStatus annotation value
      - What state is changed (cache updated, DB written, event published)
      - Test assertion patterns (assertThat response...)

  Error Paths:
    DERIVE FROM:
      - catch blocks in controller and service
      - @ExceptionHandler methods in @ControllerAdvice / @RestControllerAdvice
      - Custom exception class names and their HTTP mappings
      - Error code constants / enums
      - Failure scenario tests
    FORMAT: "[Trigger condition] → [ExceptionClass / error code] → [HTTP status]"

  Data In / Data Out:
    Reference DS-IDs and SK-IDs from Part 4 tables
    "[DS-01, DS-05] → processing → [SK-01, SK-02]"
```

